        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Exact contracts: [Spline](/api-entities-primitives-2d#spline), [CurveEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#curveevaluator), [CatmullRomEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#catmullromevaluator), [BSplineEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#bsplineevaluator), [NaturalSplineEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#naturalsplineevaluator), [HermiteEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#hermiteevaluator), [BezierEvaluator](/api-entities-primitives-2d-curves#bezierevaluator)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sampling-versus-parameter-time",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Sampling versus parameter time"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Distance sampling",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-vector-arclength"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Uniform parameter samples are not necessarily equally spaced along the curve. Length sampling is useful for roughly constant drawing speed or evenly placed markers. Adaptive flattening uses geometric error to insert more samples near curvature, while uniform subdivision is simpler and predictable."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "SubdivisionEngine adds enough local geometry for a nonlinear map to bend a shape. If only the corners of a rectangle are transformed, a curved mapping still yields straight segments between those corners. Subdivide before the deformation and choose a density based on curvature and visible resolution."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Exact contracts: [SubdivisionEngine](/api-utils#subdivisionengine), [ICurveSamplable](/api-core#interfaces), [CurveFrame](/api-core#interfaces)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "topology-and-redraw-cost",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "level": 2,
          "text": "Topology and redraw cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "tabs",
          "tabs": [
            {
              "label": "Prepare geometry",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "example",
                  "example": "labs/pg-vector-deform"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Maintain point counts and typed-array capacities when updating a curve continuously. Replacing a string, changing sample count or changing topology can trigger parsing, memory growth or triangulation. Use in-place setters where available and rebuild only when the underlying geometry needs to change."
        }
      ]
    },
